# External Flash Procedure: {{ device.vendor }} {{ device.model }}

**Device ID:** `{{ device.id }}`
**Tier:** {{ device.status.tier }} | **Score:** {{ device.status.score }}/100
**Flash Size:** {{ device.platform.flash_size_mib }} MiB ({{ device.platform.flash_package }})

## 1. Hardware Safety Checklist

1. Disconnect the AC adapter.
2. Remove the main battery and the CMOS coin battery when accessible.
3. Press and hold the power button for 10 seconds to discharge capacitors.
4. Measure the programmer VCC pin with a multimeter:
   - Standard SOIC-8 SPI flash uses **3.3 V**.
   - Low-voltage 1.8 V parts require a **1.8 V level shifter**.
   - **NEVER** connect an unmodified 5 V signal CH341A programmer to a 3.3 V or 1.8 V flash chip.
5. Verify pin 1 alignment before connecting the clip.
6. Preserve the `GbE` region (`gbe.bin`) so the Ethernet MAC address is not lost.

## 2. Mandatory 3-Read Backup

{% if dual_chip %}
This device uses **{{ device.platform.flash_chips | length }} SPI flash chips**:
{% for chip in device.platform.flash_chips %}
- **{{ chip.position }} chip**: {{ chip.size_mib }} MiB ({{ chip.package }}) — regions: {{ chip.regions | join(", ") }}
{% endfor %}

Take three identical reads of each chip and verify matching SHA-256 digests:

```bash
# Bottom 8 MiB chip (IFD, ME, GbE)
flashrom -p <programmer> -r bottom1.bin
flashrom -p <programmer> -r bottom2.bin
flashrom -p <programmer> -r bottom3.bin
sha256sum bottom1.bin bottom2.bin bottom3.bin

# Top 4 MiB chip (BIOS)
flashrom -p <programmer> -r top1.bin
flashrom -p <programmer> -r top2.bin
flashrom -p <programmer> -r top3.bin
sha256sum top1.bin top2.bin top3.bin

# Combine into a full backup image
cat bottom1.bin top1.bin > {{ device.id }}_full_backup.rom
```
{% else %}
Take three identical reads and verify matching SHA-256 digests:

```bash
flashrom -p <programmer> -r read1.bin
flashrom -p <programmer> -r read2.bin
flashrom -p <programmer> -r read3.bin
sha256sum read1.bin read2.bin read3.bin
cp read1.bin {{ device.id }}_full_backup.rom
```
{% endif %}

## 3. Flash Coreboot Image

Verify the built ROM checksum first:

```bash
cb4a verify --id {{ device.id }} --rom out/builds/{{ device.id }}/coreboot.rom
```

{% if dual_chip %}
Split the 12 MiB ROM into bottom (8 MiB) and top (4 MiB) images:

```bash
dd if=out/builds/{{ device.id }}/coreboot.rom of={{ device.id }}_bottom_new.bin bs=1M count=8
dd if=out/builds/{{ device.id }}/coreboot.rom of={{ device.id }}_top_new.bin bs=1M skip=8
```

- For a **BIOS-only** update, flash only the **4 MiB top chip**:
  ```bash
  flashrom -p <programmer> -w {{ device.id }}_top_new.bin
  ```
- For a **full** update (IFD/ME changed), flash both chips:
  ```bash
  flashrom -p <programmer> -w {{ device.id }}_bottom_new.bin
  flashrom -p <programmer> -w {{ device.id }}_top_new.bin
  ```
{% else %}
- For a **BIOS-only** image on Intel (preserves IFD, ME, and GbE):
  ```bash
  flashrom -p <programmer> --ifd -i bios -w out/builds/{{ device.id }}/coreboot.rom
  ```
- For a **full** image (built with device-specific IFD, ME, and GbE):
  ```bash
  flashrom -p <programmer> -w out/builds/{{ device.id }}/coreboot.rom
  ```
{% endif %}

Do not pass `--noverify`. See `recover.md` if the device fails to boot.
